<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Delgrès</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1766–1802</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Delgrès was a free man of colour from Martinique and an officer of the French Revolutionary army who led resistance in Guadeloupe to the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s expedition under General Richepance arrived in May 1802, he issued a proclamation of resistance and fought from Fort Saint-Charles near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basse-terre">Basse-Terre</a>. Cornered at Matouba on 28 May 1802, he and several hundred followers blew themselves up rather than surrender.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-delgres.jpg" alt="Louis Delgrès" width="320" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LPLT · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buste_Louis_Delgr%C3%A8s_%C3%A0_Petit-Bourg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
